body:
This is not a deblocking bug, it is in avc/n2avc_inter.c and it is live in the tree now. It blocks my P-picture oracle (and it silently corrupts ordinary P streams whenever an intra MB is next to a later P_Skip with non-zero neighbour MVs).

EVIDENCE
Case: 178x146 squares, QP 26, threads 4, 6 pictures, --deblock. Baseline (deblock off) is bit-exact; the deblocked stream differed only in macroblocks (7,6) and (8,6) by exactly a one-luma-sample horizontal shift. I marked frame 2 non-reference in a build-only core copy (nal_ref_idc 0, dec_ref_pic_marking omitted) and decoded with 'ffmpeg -skip_loop_filter noref', which gives the decoder's true pre-deblock picture while still using the deblocked reference for the P prediction. Result: the decoder's P_Skip MBs (7,6) and (8,6) are exact copies of the reference at MV (-4,0) quarter samples; our encoder stored and predicted (0,0). Our mbinfo for frame 2 row 6: MB (6,6) is intra (type 0, ref -1), (7,5) is P16 mv (-8,8), (8,5) P16 mv (-4,0), (7,6)/(8,6) P_Skip. For MB (7,6), A=(6,6) is intra, B=(7,5) mv (-8,8); the decoder's 8.4.1.3 median gives (-4,0). Our n2avc_inter_skip_mv forces (0,0) because neighbour_inter() returns 0 for an intra A, so !a_ok fires.

THE SPEC (ITU-T H.264 11/2007, clause 8.4.1.1, page 147, verbatim): 'If any of the following conditions are true, both components of the motion vector mvL0 are set equal to 0. - mbAddrA is not available - mbAddrB is not available - refIdxL0A is equal to 0 and both components of mvL0A are equal to 0 - refIdxL0B is equal to 0 and both components of mvL0B are equal to 0'. There is NO 'A or B is intra' condition. An intra neighbour has refIdxL0 -1 and must fall through to 8.4.1.3, where it is available with refIdx -1 and mv (0,0); the median / 'exactly one' rules then apply. So the lead's original edit was right and WP04's commit 31bca07 reverted to a rule that is not in the Recommendation. I checked the printed clause text, not another implementation.

FIX (in n2avc_inter_skip_mv, avc/n2avc_inter.c -- WP04's file, I did not touch it):
  if (!n2avc_avail(s, mbx-1, mby) || !n2avc_avail(s, mbx, mby-1)) { *mvx=*mvy=0; return; }
  a = &s->mb[mby*s->mb_w + mbx-1]; b = &s->mb[(mby-1)*s->mb_w + mbx];
  if ((a->ref==0 && a->mvx==0 && a->mvy==0) || (b->ref==0 && b->mvx==0 && b->mvy==0)) { *mvx=*mvy=0; return; }
  n2avc_inter_pmv(s, mbx, mby, mvx, mvy);
(availability is the 6.4.9 sense used by n2avc_avail: inside the picture and the same slice.)

PROOF: a build-directory copy of n2avc_inter.c with exactly that change (+ the core deblock integration) makes 178x146 squares QP26 t4 P bit-exact against ffmpeg with deblocking on. I do NOT edit the file; my run_tests.sh applies the same patch to a build copy and says so in STATUS.md, so the R2 oracle can be judged now. Please have WP04 (or you) apply it to the real file; WP04's own harness's literal test at t_inter.c:522 encodes the wrong rule and should be corrected with the clause text above.

My interface proposal and the 3 core edits (open, slice header idc 0 + two offsets, deblock call) are unchanged and still needed in avc2/n2avc_core.c. The --deblock CLI flag for WP10 is still missing (avc/test/core/n2avc_cli.c, WP03-owned); my suite uses its own t_cli.
